I thought “non-centered parameterization” was someone’s fancy word for what we called the Matt trick.

> On Oct 2, 2015, at 11:09 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>
>
>> On Oct 2, 2015, at 10:57 PM, Andrew Gelman <Gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
>>
>> Hi, I have 2 thoughts:
>>
>> 1.  In a program such as stan_lme which must set up the model automatically, we could have 2 models, one where all variance components have the centered paraemterization, the other where all variance components have the noncentered parameterization.  And Stan could alternate between these.  This might be a safer option than just doing one or the other. (If we just do one, it’s my impression that the noncentered parameterization is safer in that things can sometimes go really really wrong with the centered parameterization.)
>>
>> 2.  What about a model that includes both as special cases, for example:
>>
>> theta ~ normal(0,sigma_theta);
>> for (n in 1:N)
>> y_pred[n] <- mu + tau_theta*theta[group[n]];
>> y ~ normal(y_pred, sigma_y);
>>
>> In this case, if you set sigma_theta <- 1 you get the noncentered parameterization and if you set tau_theta <- 1 you get the centered parameterization.  So one option would be to put some pseudo-prior on sigma_theta and sigma_tau, something that could allow the best of both in that it could allow the HMC to “find” the smoother regions of space.
>
> I'm a bit confused by the terminology here.  I thought
> of the centering as a matter of where you put the mean,
> either in the linear predictor as an intercept or in the prior.
> That is, "centered" meant "centered around the prior mean"
> and non-centered meant "centered around zero".
>
> What Andrew's talking about is the scaling part.  I'm
> not sure what kind of priors would make that model work,
> because now you have theta, sigma_theta and tau_theta
> all tied together.
>
> Anyway, I think of the centering/non-centering as this:
>
>  Centered:
>
>  theta ~ normal(mu, sigma_theta);
>  ...
>    y_pred[n] <- theta[group[n]] + ...
>
>
>  Non-Centered:
>
>  theta ~ normal(0, sigma_theta);
>  ...
>    y_pred[n] <- mu + theta[group[n]] + ...
>
> - Bob
>
>> Could this work?
>>
>> A
>>
>>> On Oct 2, 2015, at 3:00 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>
>>> Mathematically it’s straightforward —think of an extended
>>> state space of (centered parameters, non-centered parameters)
>>> then
>>>
>>> \int dnon-centered parameters p(centered parameters, non-centered parameters)
>>> = p(centered parameters)
>>>
>>> The point I was trying to make is doing this automatically in
>>> practice is a _whole_ nother matter.
>>>
>>> On Oct 2, 2015, at 7:51 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>>
>>>> Thanks for clarifying.  It's what I was fumbling toward
>>>> with "different parameters".
>>>>
>>>> If we consider transformed params, they have the same
>>>> posteriors.  But they sample over different subsets of them.
>>>> But those subsets are easily transformed back and forth.
>>>>
>>>> So I don't see any reason why we couldn't jump back and
>>>> forth in some kind of specialized MCMC that randomized
>>>> which stepper to use.  Or does that not define a proper Markov
>>>> chain?  I don't see why not.  It's all kinds of crazy w.r.t.
>>>> HMC because it's a lower dimensional given the deterministic
>>>> relationship between some of the "parameters".
>>>>
>>>> - Bob
>>>>
>>>>> On Oct 2, 2015, at 2:16 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>> One is free to use a centered or non-centered parameterization
>>>>> for each _group_, but within a group that choice must be consistent.
>>>>> This gets even uglier when you have hierarchical models with many
>>>>> layers — if you think about the hierarchical model as a tree then you
>>>>> can choose between the two parameterizations at every node.  Using
>>>>> both parameterizations for different groups is straightforward, if a bit
>>>>> ungainly.
>>>>>
>>>>> Now if you’re talking about switching between the parameterizations
>>>>> after Markov transitions then everything becomes significantly more
>>>>> complicated.  The problem is that centered and non-centered
>>>>> parameterizations aren’t really parameterizations — they’re identifying
>>>>> different target spaces all together.  In other words, they require
>>>>> different parameter blocks.
>>>>>
>>>>> Say beta is the centered parameter,
>>>>>
>>>>> beta ~ normal(mu, sigma),
>>>>>
>>>>> and beta_tilde is the non-centered parameter,
>>>>>
>>>>> beta_tilde ~ normal(0, 1),
>>>>> beta = mu + sigma * beta_tilde.
>>>>>
>>>>> In order to interleave transitions using each you’d have to do something
>>>>> like
>>>>>
>>>>> transition_using_beta
>>>>> ...
>>>>> transition_using_beta_tilde
>>>>> beta = mu + sigma * beta_tilde
>>>>> …
>>>>> transition_using_beta
>>>>> ...
>>>>>
>>>>> And so on.  In Stan this would essentially reduce to switching between
>>>>> parameters and transformed parameters and a giant rabbit hole of how
>>>>> to identify what to switch and how to switch.  And all of that assumes
>>>>> that we can even identify what groups should be in what parameterization,
>>>>> which is beyond our current capabilities — we can identify when at least
>>>>> one group is poorly parameterized, but which group.
>>>>>
>>>>> So, um, good luck?
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>> On Oct 2, 2015, at 6:49 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>>> [moved to stan-dev]
>>>>>>
>>>>>> Backstory:  we're evaluating IRT models in Stan vs. Stata and
>>>>>> Daniel Furr (at Berkeley, working with Sophia Rabe-Hesketh) switched
>>>>>> the parameterization from centered to non-centered at Andrew's behest
>>>>>> and found sampling slowed down by a factor of four or so (see below
>>>>>> for details).  Andrew wanted to stick with non-centered as our
>>>>>> recommended parameterization even in this example.  But that's not
>>>>>> what this mail's about, it's about the follow on.
>>>>>>
>>>>>> Andrew's been talking about combining centered and non-centered parameterizations
>>>>>> in a single model.  I recall something like that in a paper by XL et al. for
>>>>>> Metropolis or Gibbs.
>>>>>>
>>>>>> What I don't understand is how you would "switch back and forth between them"
>>>>>> if you compiled them separately.  They have different parameters.
>>>>>>
>>>>>> Now I could imagine something that looked like this:
>>>>>>
>>>>>> alpha[n] <- (1 - lambda) * gamma + beta[jj[n]]
>>>>>>
>>>>>> where
>>>>>>
>>>>>> beta ~ normal(lambda * gamma, sigma);
>>>>>>
>>>>>> lambda ~ uniform(0, 1);
>>>>>>
>>>>>> So lambda would control how much centering (lambda = 0 is non-centered,
>>>>>> lambda = 1 is centered).  I think the likelihood is the same for all
>>>>>> lambda in [0, 1], so we'd need some way to prefer the one that works
>>>>>> more efficiently.  Which seems like something we have to do outside
>>>>>> the Stan language.
>>>>>>
>>>>>> - Bob
>>>>>>
>>>>>>> On Oct 1, 2015, at 7:09 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
>>>>>>>
>>>>>>> The idea would be to compile both models and then switch back and forth between them.  Or, better still, but I don’t know how exactly to do it, would be to fit a 3rd, larger model that includes the 2 models as special cases and then there’d be a new hyperparameter.  But I agree that this is off-topic for this discussion.
>>>>>>>
>>>>>>> It was my understanding that stan_lme (or whatever it’s now called) automatically uses the noncentered parameterization so I think of it as our default.
>>>>>>>
>>>>>>> A
>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>> On Oct 1, 2015, at 5:36 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>> It's only our recommended coding strategy when there's relatively
>>>>>>>> little data.  Otherwise, we've been recommending the non-centered
>>>>>>>> parameterization.
>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>> This is way off topic for this paper, but I don't see how we
>>>>>>>> could  go back and forth between two different densities.  Stan
>>>>>>>> samples over the unconstrained parameterizations, and these are
>>>>>>>> different between the centered and non-centered parameterizations,
>>>>>>>> which is why they behave differently.
>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>> - Bob
>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>> On Oct 1, 2015, at 4:45 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>> Hi, it’s a bummer that it’s slower this way, but given that this is our recommended coding strategy I think it’s best to use this coding and just accept the hit in efficiency and runtime.  Longer-term I think we want to develop an algorithm that goes back and forth between the two parameterizations but we’re not there yet, so for now I think it makes sense to go with this new coding.
>>>>>>>>> A
>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> On Oct 1, 2015, at 1:30 PM, Daniel Furr <danie...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> Okay, I've made these changes and pushed them to Github. This appears to make the sampling of the variance parameters worse though. For example, n_eff/sec for the SDs is now 7.45 and 5.71 for the hierarchical rasch model, but these were 16.97 and 28.71 in a previous draft. For the simpler rasch model the difference is 13.74 versus 46.68 for the one SD parameter.
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> On Wed, Sep 30, 2015 at 7:32 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
>>>>>>>>>> Hi, Bob.  No, I think we should use the Matt trick; so for model 1:
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> model {
>>>>>>>>>> vector[N] y_pred;
>>>>>>>>>> theta ~ normal(0, 1);
>>>>>>>>>> for (n in 1:N)
>>>>>>>>>> y_pred[n] <- sigma_theta*theta[pp[n]] + delta[ii[n]];
>>>>>>>>>> y ~ bernoulli_logit(y_pred);
>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> I’m using the notation y_pred rather than eta because I think it’s clearer.
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> And for model 2:
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> model {
>>>>>>>>>> vector[N] y_pred;
>>>>>>>>>> theta ~ normal(0, 1);
>>>>>>>>>> delta ~ normal(0, 1);
>>>>>>>>>> for (n in 1:N)
>>>>>>>>>> eta[n] <- mu + sigma_theta*theta[pp[n]] + sigma_delta*delta[ii[n]];
>>>>>>>>>> y ~ bernoulli_logit(y_pred);
>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> Does this make sense?  Even if it does not make much of a difference I think it’s better because it exemplifies good practice.
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> On Sep 29, 2015, at 3:58 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> Andrew:
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> Are you OK with the models Daniel (Furr) is using for comparison?
>>>>>>>>>>> Here they are.  I think I can translate to JAGS using dflat(),
>>>>>>>>>>> but I want to make sure that's OK with you before producing the final
>>>>>>>>>>> plots for the paper.
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> - Bob
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> BASIC
>>>>>>>>>>> -----
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> data {
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1> N;
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1> I;
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1> P;
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1, upper=I> ii[N];
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1, upper=P> pp[N];
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=0, upper=1> y[N];
>>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>> parameters {
>>>>>>>>>>> real<lower=0> sigma;
>>>>>>>>>>> vector[I] delta;
>>>>>>>>>>> vector[P] theta;
>>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>> model {
>>>>>>>>>>> vector[N] eta;
>>>>>>>>>>> theta ~ normal(0, sigma);
>>>>>>>>>>> for (n in 1:N)
>>>>>>>>>>> eta[n] <- theta[pp[n]] + delta[ii[n]];
>>>>>>>>>>> y ~ bernoulli_logit(eta);
>>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> HIERARCHICAL
>>>>>>>>>>> ------------
>>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>> data {
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1> N;
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1> I;
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1> P;
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1, upper=I> ii[N];
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=1, upper=P> pp[N];
>>>>>>>>>>> int<lower=0, upper=1> y[N];
>>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>> parameters {
>>>>>>>>>>> real<lower=0> sigma;
>>>>>>>>>>> real<lower=0> tau;
>>>>>>>>>>> real mu;
>>>>>>>>>>> vector[I] delta;
>>>>>>>>>>> vector[P] theta;
>>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>> model {
>>>>>>>>>>> vector[N] eta;
>>>>>>>>>>> theta ~ normal(0, sigma);
>>>>>>>>>>> delta ~ normal(0, tau);
>>>>>>>>>>> for (n in 1:N)
>>>>>>>>>>> eta[n] <- mu + theta[pp[n]] + delta[ii[n]];
>>>>>>>>>>> y ~ bernoulli_logit(eta);
>>>>>>>>>>> }
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>
