Yes, that seems reasonable.  It's a drag, though, that it's getting
harder and harder with each passing year to contribute to Stan.  I'm
finding the current process pretty overwhelming myself and it's
really slowing down my development progress.  This'll just make matters
worse on that front.  But I don't see an alternative --- we can't just have
changes to Stan break CmdStan, RStan, and PyStan.

> - should we have some way of specifying the upstream job's branch? (a hacky way would be to write into some hidden text file that's removed before merging.)

Yes, but can't we somehow do it as part of the pull request rather than as
a hack on the branch itself?  I find it particularly confusing how the manuals
and doxygen blink into and out of existence on the branches.

> - is there another way to deal with this?
>
> An actual use case: https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/pull/1630
> This causes CmdStan to fail to build due to a missing source file. It's not a hard fix, just want to know how we should handle it.

You mean that if this branch was merged into Stan, then CmdStan would
stop working because it's missing something?

- Bob

